; The golden-corpus harness vocabulary — the shape of a case's
; `times.sjon` (P107, plan 09, "The golden corpus"; pacer's
; `schema/pacer-corpus.sjon` idiom).
;
; A harness file is NOT a timeline document: it is an ordered script of
; queries and verbs the runner replays against the case's `doc.sjon`,
; `song.mid` or `song.it`. `src/corpus_runner.zig` embeds this manifest
; via the `corpus_schema` build import and PRELOADS it once for the whole
; walk, exactly as `src/Schema.zig` preloads the timeline vocabulary — two
; disjoint vocabularies, two bundles, NEVER composed into one. A harness
; file is not a document and shares no form with one, so composing the two
; would only put every document's vocabulary within reach of a typo in a
; `times.sjon` (and, until 2026-09-21, made every unqualified `(timing …)`
; `ambiguous_form` in both: the it tier declares one for its header section
; and this manifest declared one for a verb — see the note below). One
; project, one vocabulary; the harness files trade editor support for the
; document's, which is why this manifest is deliberately absent from
; `sjon-project.sjon`.
;
; 2026-09-21 — the `timing` verb is GONE from this manifest. It echoed the
; derived TimeMap for the studio's timing provider; pstudio is not a host,
; nothing ever implemented it, and the runner answered `UNRUN timing` and
; FAILED any case that asked — a declared verb that could only ever fail is
; not a vocabulary, it is a trap. `(echo …)` already carries the whole
; derivation at the top of every document case, which is what the verb
; would have printed.
;
; What lives HERE is SHAPE. What stays in the runner is everything a
; schema cannot see: document order (the script runs top to bottom), the
; WIDTH of every hex bit pattern (16 digits for an f64), which verbs a
; case's fixtures make meaningful, and every tolerance. The seconds are
; strings on purpose, pacer's reasoning verbatim: `sjon fmt` rewrites a
; hex LITERAL to decimal, so `0x3FE0000000000000` in a harness file is one
; formatter pass from destruction, while a string is inert under it and
; carries its own width.
;
; No `:version`, for the reason `schema/tempura.sjon` declares none.

(plugin :name tempura-corpus
  :description "The golden corpus's harness vocabulary: the ordered script of queries and verbs a case replays — the format cores' read, round trip, walk and write, then positions, snaps, edit plans, the score export, the built file and the re-dump."

  ; ---- value kinds ----

  (value-kind :name f64-bits
    :description "A seconds value as a raw f64 bit pattern, 16 hex digits: \"3FE0000000000000\" is 0.5. A string, not a literal: it survives `sjon fmt`, carries its own width, and lets a case pin a tie, a NaN or an infinity without a float parser in the loop. The runner checks the width."
    :underlying string)

  (value-kind :name tick-value
    :description "A position in tempura ticks (TPQ = 46126080 per quarter note), 0..2^53. Past MAX_TICK is a tick_overflow diagnostic, which is itself a thing a case pins — so the ceiling here is the grid's, and the refusal is the runner's to observe."
    :underlying number
    :numeric (numeric-bounds :min 0 :max 9007199254740992 :integer true)
    :unit (unit-shape :reject true))

  (value-kind :name snap-grid
    :description "A snap subdivision, spelled as `Playhead.SnapGrid` names it: `q4` is a quarter, `q8` an eighth, `q12` a triplet eighth, `q16` a sixteenth, `q32` a thirty-second, and `beat` and `bar` are the meter's own lines. A member set rather than a free string, because the set is closed in Zig and a subdivision the playhead cannot name is a typo, not a ratio."
    :underlying symbol
    :members (member-set
      (member :name q4 :description "A quarter note.")
      (member :name q8 :description "An eighth note.")
      (member :name q12 :description "A triplet eighth — TPQ / 3, an integer on this grid.")
      (member :name q16 :description "A sixteenth note.")
      (member :name q32 :description "A thirty-second note.")
      (member :name beat :description "The meter's own beat, 4 / d quarters.")
      (member :name bar :description "The meter's own bar lines.")))

  (value-kind :name row-cap
    :description "A cap on how many tracker rows a walk may play before it gives up, 1..2^20. The walk's own default is the ceiling, so a case that wants the runaway diagnostic asks for a small one; the cap is a walk INPUT, and the `walk_runaway` it trips is a diagnostic the golden pins."
    :underlying number
    :numeric (numeric-bounds :min 1 :max 1048576 :integer true)
    :unit (unit-shape :reject true))

  (value-kind :name build-kind
    :description "Which file a (build …) writes."
    :underlying symbol
    :members (member-set
      (member :name mid :description "A Standard MIDI File — the extension the file carries, so a case reads as the file it writes.")
      (member :name it :description "An Impulse Tracker module.")))

  (value-kind :name cc-number
    :description "A MIDI controller number, 0..127 — which controller a native curve lane renders onto when a (build …) writes a .mid (plan 08's --cc). A curve lane with none is render_cc_missing and rides in the residue alone, which is itself a golden worth pinning."
    :underlying number
    :numeric (numeric-bounds :min 0 :max 127 :integer true)
    :unit (unit-shape :reject true))

  (value-kind :name plan-op
    :description "Which edit operation to plan. Deliberately an unconstrained symbol: the operation set is the edit engine's and is enumerated there, so an unknown one is a runner diagnostic naming the case rather than a schema error in a file nobody edits by hand."
    :underlying symbol)

  ; ---- the script, executed in document order ----
  ;
  ; The four format-core verbs first (P107, the cores half): they take a
  ; case whose fixture is `song.mid` or `song.it` and need no document.

  (form :name read
    :description "Read the case's fixture with the core its name selects — `song.mid` through the SMF reader, `song.it` through the IT one — and echo what came back: the header fields and the track, event and record counts, then every diagnostic with its numeric code, severity and span. Takes no rows.")

  (form :name roundtrip
    :description "The two fidelity rungs of plan 09 over the case's fixture. Rung 1 is `write(read(bytes)) == bytes`, which holds for a file tempura's own canonical writer produced; rung 2 is `read(write(read(bytes)))` equal to `read(bytes)` event for event for a `.mid` (every `at`, kind and active payload, foreign chunks included) and field for field for an `.it` (header, orders, patterns cell by cell, every pass-through region byte-equal). A FOREIGN fixture — one the canonical writer would never have produced, a SMPTE division or a command byte no IT effect names — is what rung 2 is for, and its golden pins `(rung1 :ok false)` as a fact the case's own `times.sjon` explains: the verdict is the golden's byte-equality, never the flag's value. Takes no rows.")

  (form :name walk
    :description "Echo the IT timing walk's `RowMap`: one row per row PLAYED with its start and length, the tempo segments, the note onsets, the total and the loop point, then the walk's diagnostics. An `.it` fixture only: it is the tracker's own row clock, and an SMF conductor has no rows. What an SMF conductor derives is in the `(echo …)` header every document case opens with."
    (key :name max-rows :type row-cap :optional true :default 1048576
      :description "Stop the walk after this many rows and raise `walk_runaway`. The default is the walk's own ceiling, so only a runaway case states it."))

  (form :name write
    :description "Write the case's fixture back through the canonical writer with the switches below, and echo the bytes' sha256, their length and the event count. What `(roundtrip)` asserts, this verb SHOWS: a layout change is a diff in the golden, and the hash is what x86_64, aarch64 and wasm32 must agree on."
    (key :name format0 :type boolean :optional true :default false
      :description "`--format 0`: merge every track into one, by tick, stable, the first track's events first at a tie. SMF only."))

  (form :name times
    :description "One Playhead.at per positional seconds value: the tick, bar, beat, sub-tick, the sections in force and the surrounding cues, after the end-behaviour wrap — and, for a document an IT anchor conducts, the tracker position beside them. The seconds are BIT PATTERNS, which is what lets a case ask for a segment boundary and the ulp below it in the same breath. May repeat and interleave with the other script forms."
    :positional f64-bits)

  (form :name at
    :description "The same answer as `times`, asked by TICK rather than by seconds: the query an editor makes when the playhead was dragged on the grid instead of on the clock."
    (key :name tick :type tick-value
      :description "Where to ask."))

  (form :name snap
    :description "One Playhead.snap: the nearest grid line under the meter in force, with the tie broken downwards."
    (key :name tick :type tick-value
      :description "The tick to snap.")
    (key :name grid :type snap-grid
      :description "The subdivision to snap to.")
    (key :name swing :type f64-bits :optional true :default "0000000000000000"
      :description "How far the odd grid lines are pushed late, as a fraction of the step and as a BIT PATTERN: the offset rounds through the one literal seam, so the last bit of the fraction is exactly what a case wants to pin. The default is +0.0 — a straight grid."))

  (form :name plan
    :description "Plan one edit operation and render its actions as text, so a change in ordering is a diff rather than a surprise. Plans only — the corpus never applies an edit it did not print first."
    :positional plan-op
    (key :name args :type any :optional true :walk-opaque true
      :description "The operation's arguments, in the edit engine's own vocabulary. Opaque to this schema: the engine checks them, with spans."))

  (form :name export-score
    :description "Export the case's document as a pacer score and render the text's hash and its first lines. Takes no rows — the whole document is the input. The easing hints a stale curve lane kept (plan 04, rule 2) come from the case's own reconciliation when its fixture is a file."
    (key :name lines :type tick-value :optional true :default 12
      :description "How many leading lines of the exported score to render beside the hash.")
    (key :name max-keys :type tick-value :optional true :default 512
      :description "The per-track share of pacer's key budget (plan 08, \"The key budget\"; the CLI's --max-keys-per-track). The default is the exporter's own, and a case lowers it so the budget warning can be pinned over a lane a reviewer can read rather than over six hundred keyframes nobody will."))

  (form :name build
    :description "Build the case's document into a file of the given kind and render the bytes' length and hash, then rung 1 over what was written. An ANCHORED document is its inline tier through the canonical writer; a NATIVE one goes through plan 08's renderers and plan 04's carriers, and the golden carries rung 3 — the dump of what was built, against the document that built it — beside rung 1. Same document must give the same bytes on x86_64, aarch64 and wasm32 — that is what the hash is for."
    (key :name kind :type build-kind
      :description "Which file to write.")
    (key :name cc :type cc-number :optional true
      :description "Which controller a native curve lane renders onto (plan 08's --cc). Only a .mid reads it."))

  (form :name dump
    :description "Re-dump the case's document and render its timeline tier, so a read-write round trip is a diff. Takes no rows."))
